content.text:
I fixed the bug: in scoped styles, declarations sitting directly inside an `@media` or `@container` block nested in a rule now get the scope attribute. The existing compiler-sfc tests pass (412 passed, 1 skipped).

**Cause:** When a scoped rule contains nested rules, the compiler moves the rule's own declarations into a `& { … }` wrapper and adds the scope attribute there. It did not do the same for declarations inside at-rules nested in that rule. So the `@media` declarations had less specificity than the root `background-color` and lost to it.

**Fix** (in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`): I pulled the wrapping logic into a small helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and also on each at-rule directly inside it, unless the rule is a `:deep` rule.

For the input `.foo { background-color: red; .bar {} @media … { background-color: green; .bar { color: white } } }`, the output is now:

```css
.foo {
  &[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
  .bar[data-v-test] {}
  @media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
    &[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
    .bar[data-v-test] { color: white }
  }
}
```

That matches the output the issue asks for. I checked it with a throwaway test file, which I deleted afterwards, and I didn't add a permanent test.

Only at-rules one level below the rule are handled. Declarations in a second at-rule nested inside the first (e.g. `@supports` within `@media`) still won't be wrapped.

